WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.127 --> 00:00:03.147
A recall lets voters remove an elected official

2
00:00:03.147 --> 00:00:04.667
before the term ends.

3
00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:07.447
Proposition five changes what happens next.

4
00:00:08.007 --> 00:00:10.147
It does not eliminate the power to recall

5
00:00:10.147 --> 00:00:11.187
state officials.

6
00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:14.300
Proposition five removes the replacement question.

7
00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.660
The office would be filled through succession, appointment,

8
00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:20.740
or a separate election, depending on the office

9
00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:22.600
and when the recall happens.

10
00:00:22.893 --> 00:00:25.533
If a legislator is recalled, a special election

11
00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:27.793
would generally fill the seat.

12
00:00:28.213 --> 00:00:31.113
Vacancies in most other state offices would be

13
00:00:31.113 --> 00:00:33.273
filled by gubernatorial appointment.

14
00:00:33.573 --> 00:00:36.173
A voter might want removal considered on its

15
00:00:36.173 --> 00:00:38.773
own, rather than as a shortcut for a

16
00:00:38.773 --> 00:00:40.393
candidate with limited support.

17
00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:43.920
Another voter could object to giving appointments or

18
00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.480
automatic succession a larger role.

19
00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:48.740
Yes changes the replacement process;

20
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.460
no keeps the replacement question on recall ballots.

21
00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:54.680
Visit our website for the fuller explanation,

22
00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.720
source links, and official voter guide.
